---
import CaseStudyLayout from '@components/CaseStudyLayout.astro';
import StatCallout from '@components/StatCallout.astro';
---

<CaseStudyLayout
  title="Dual-Enrollment Impact Analysis"
  subtitle="A causal inference study of a state dual-enrollment program using linked K-12 and postsecondary administrative data, built with propensity score matching and Bayesian hierarchical models."
  category="Causal Inference"
  tools={['R', 'brms', 'Stan', 'dplyr', 'ggplot2', 'MatchIt']}
  duration="Professional work · 2023 – 2024"
  ogDescription="Bayesian causal inference on education administrative data: propensity score matching and hierarchical models to address selection bias."
>

  <!-- OBJECTIVE -->
  <section>
    <p class="cs-section-label">Objective</p>
    <h2 class="cs-section-title">Estimating a program's effect when participation isn't random</h2>
    <div class="cs-prose">
      <p>
        Dual-enrollment programs let high school students take college courses before they
        graduate. Students who choose to participate tend to differ from those who don't, so
        simply comparing outcomes would mix up the program's effect with who chose to enroll.
      </p>
      <p>
        The goal was to estimate the program's effect on postsecondary outcomes credibly,
        accounting for selection bias and for differences between student groups.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr class="cs-divider" />

  <!-- DATA SOURCES -->
  <section>
    <p class="cs-section-label">Data Sources</p>
    <h2 class="cs-section-title">Linked administrative records</h2>
    <div class="cs-prose">
      <p>
        The analysis used state education administrative data linked to national
        postsecondary enrollment records, which makes it possible to follow students after they
        leave the K-12 system. The data was accessed under a data-sharing agreement, so this
        write-up covers the approach rather than the data or results.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr class="cs-divider" />

  <!-- METHODOLOGY -->
  <section>
    <p class="cs-section-label">Methodology</p>
    <h2 class="cs-section-title">Propensity score matching + Bayesian hierarchical logistic regression</h2>
    <div class="cs-prose">
      <p>
        Two complementary methods address the selection problem.
      </p>

      <h3 class="font-sans font-semibold text-white/90 text-base mt-6 mb-2">Step 1 — Propensity score matching</h3>
      <p>
        Estimate each student's probability of participating from pre-treatment covariates,
        then match participants to similar non-participants to build a balanced comparison
        group. Covariate balance is checked with standardized mean differences before and
        after matching.
      </p>
    </div>

    <pre class="cs-code"># Propensity score matching with MatchIt
library(MatchIt)

match_out <- matchit(
  treated ~ prior_achievement + demographics + school + cohort,
  data     = students,
  method   = "nearest",
  distance = "logit",
  caliper  = 0.05
)</pre>

    <div class="cs-prose">
      <h3 class="font-sans font-semibold text-white/90 text-base mt-6 mb-2">Step 2 — Bayesian hierarchical logistic regression</h3>
      <p>
        On the matched sample, a Bayesian hierarchical logistic regression (brms / Stan)
        estimates the effect on binary postsecondary outcomes. Schools enter as random
        effects because students in the same school are not independent, and interaction
        terms test whether effects differ across student groups. The Bayesian approach gives
        full posterior distributions rather than a single point estimate, which makes
        uncertainty explicit.
      </p>
    </div>

    <pre class="cs-code"># Hierarchical logistic model with brms
library(brms)

model <- brm(
  outcome ~ treated * group + covariates + (1 | school),
  data   = matched_data,
  family = bernoulli(link = "logit"),
  prior  = c(
    prior(normal(0, 1.5), class = "b"),
    prior(exponential(1), class = "sd")
  ),
  chains = 4, cores = 4
)</pre>
  </section>

  <hr class="cs-divider" />

  <!-- SKILLS -->
  <section>
    <h2 class="cs-section-title">Causal reasoning on real administrative data</h2>
    <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-3 gap-6">
      <StatCallout value="Causal" label="Selection-bias-aware design" sublabel="Propensity score matching" size="sm" />
      <StatCallout value="Bayesian" label="Hierarchical modeling" sublabel="brms / Stan" size="sm" />
      <StatCallout value="Linked" label="Multi-system data" sublabel="K-12 + postsecondary" size="sm" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr class="cs-divider" />

  <!-- CHALLENGES -->
  <section>
    <p class="cs-section-label">Challenges & Tradeoffs</p>
    <h2 class="cs-section-title">Limits of the approach</h2>
    <div class="cs-prose">
      <p>
        <strong class="text-white/85">Matching trades sample size for validity.</strong> Matching
        with a caliper drops participants who have no sufficiently similar comparison, which
        makes the estimate more internally valid but less representative of all participants.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong class="text-white/85">Unobserved confounders remain a limitation.</strong> Matching
        only controls for measured covariates. Students who opt in may differ in unmeasured
        ways such as motivation or family support, so sensitivity analysis matters when
        interpreting any result.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong class="text-white/85">Bayesian computation is slow.</strong> Hierarchical models
        with interactions are expensive to sample, which affects how often an analysis like
        this can be refreshed.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr class="cs-divider" />

  <!-- LINKS -->
  <section>
    <p class="cs-section-label">Links</p>
    <h2 class="cs-section-title">Publication & code</h2>
    <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-4">
      <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 font-mono text-sm text-white/25 border border-white/10 px-4 py-2.5 rounded cursor-not-allowed">
        Not public — data-sharing agreement
      </span>
    </div>
  </section>

</CaseStudyLayout>
